, replace subjective terms with observable outcomes, and state how each is verified.","tier":"DET","validity":1.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State in the Approach/Scope that the KNOWN_PROVIDER_NAMES membership check for a configured `provider` runs FIRST inside the `if provider:` block — ahead of the existing `qualifier == provider` early-return and the conflict branch — so a typo'd provider is rejected even when it matches an inline qualifier.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"indeterminate","drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: '_resolve_target(model, provider) validates provider against the same set and raises LLMConfigError naming the offending value ... before composing anything.'","Existing code (model_classes.py:238-250) checks `if qualifier == provider: return model` as the FIRST sub-branch inside `if provider:`, returning EARLY before any composition; a conflict `raise` is the second branch.","For `_resolve_target(\"bedrok:x\", \"bedrok\")` the `qualifier == provider` early-return fires and the typo is never caught unless the membership check is placed AHEAD of that branch — which the plan's stated goal ('report the operator's typo where they made it') requires but the plan does not pin.","'before composing anything' constrains only branch (c), not the early-return (a) or conflict (b) branches."],"finding":"The plan specifies WHAT `_resolve_target` validates (configured provider against KNOWN_PROVIDER_NAMES, raising LLMConfigError) but not WHERE the check sits relative to the existing `if provider:` sub-branches, and the placement changes behavior for the motivating typo case.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f6f11e05e83f79ff2","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach → 'Two paths, deliberately different' item 2; Scope → src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py; model_classes.py:238-250","norm_id":"n33574051f68b420e","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","scenarios":["An operator writes a typo'd provider that also appears as the inline qualifier, e.g. model=`bedrok:claude-opus-4-8`, provider=`bedrok`: with the check placed after the early-return it slips through unvalidated."],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"In `_resolve_target`, add `if provider and provider not in KNOWN_PROVIDER_NAMES: raise LLMConfigError(...)` as the very first statement of the `if provider:` block (before `split_provider_qualifier`), and say so explicitly in the plan so the raise precedes both the `qualifier == provider` return and the conflict raise.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.75,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"","cohort":null,"criteria":["E4"],"decision":"indeterminate","drop_reason":null,"evidence":[],"finding":"The Acceptance-Criterion verification command `grep -rn '\":\" in model' src/rebar/llm/` is asserted to return 'no matches', but pre-existing eval-only code in src/rebar/llm/evals/eval.py lines 101-102 contains the exact substring `\":\" in model`, so the grep will return two hits and the AC's stated verification result is false. The parent ticket 03b0's own implementation comment explicitly left evals/eval.py's colon splits alone as intentional ('eval.py:101-102 is eval-only'), so this is not new work — the grep predicate is simply mis-scoped to prove 'both deciders route through split_provider_qualifier'.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f86114b8586b9d001","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria — 'Both deciders still route through the one shared split_provider_qualifier' bullet","norm_id":"n2e857fc6355b47ba","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","scenarios":[],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"Replace the verification with `grep -rn '\":\" in model' src/rebar/llm/config.py src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py returning no matches` (the two decider modules), and keep the positive check that _resolve_target and infer_provider each call split_provider_qualifier.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"","cohort":null,"criteria":["G1G2"],"decision":"indeterminate","drop_reason":null,"evidence":[],"finding":"The plan's KNOWN_PROVIDER_NAMES set (the enumerated 24 names) excludes `google_genai`, but an existing test the plan lists as unchanged uses a `google_genai:` qualifier and will break under the new membership rule.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fe2f6445a4dec071e","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Scope / Testing sections; tests/unit/test_provider_qualifier.py:72,75-79","norm_id":"n31908d4a4df188df","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","scenarios":[],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"Add `google_genai` to the KNOWN_PROVIDER_NAMES set and update the '24 names' count/enumeration accordingly, so the existing shape-accepted names the current tests cover (google_genai, openai-responses) keep resolving; alternatively, scope in the edit to test_provider_qualifier.py:72/75 with a stated behavior change.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"","cohort":null,"criteria":["project.symbol-reference-completeness"],"decision":"indeterminate","drop_reason":null,"evidence":[],"finding":"The plan's `03b0` regression inventory lists `tests/unit/test_provider_qualifier.py` line 75 among cases that 'must keep passing with no edits', but line 75 (`test_an_already_qualified_string_is_returned_unchanged`) is parametrized over `google_genai:gemini-2.0` (line 72), and the behavior change to `split_provider_qualifier` breaks that parametrization: `google_genai` is not a member of the 24-name `KNOWN_PROVIDER_NAMES` set (the plan's own out-of-scope note states `google_genai` 'is in neither registry'). The affected-test inventory omits that this existing case will need editing, contradicting the plan's claim it passes unedited.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":n